#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616} :activity/id #uuid "c4ddd229-d7db-5b1a-a201-7aaf8cc88aac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088995 :tutorial/content "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "1471f79a-59cb-50b0-b82f-f640e329bb7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9720 :tutorial/title "Connection With Polynomial Division"} :step/id #uuid "f91395f3-3117-5229-a814-b5ebada7f90c" :step/math-academy-id 191472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088933 :tutorial/content "If we know a *root* of a polynomial, we can immediately determine a *factor* of the polynomial using the **factor theorem**. \n\nThe factor theorem states that if $p(x)$ is a polynomial and $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a *root* of $p(x),$ meaning that \n\n$$\np({\\color{blue}{r}})=0\n$$\n\nthen $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a *factor* of $p(x).$\n\nThe converse of the factor theorem is also true. In other words, if $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a factor of $p(x),$ then $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a root of the polynomial.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2.\n$$\n\nNote that $x = {\\color{blue}2}$ is a root of this polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & ={2}^{3}-2(2)^{2}-2+2 \\\\  & =8-8-2+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the factor theorem, $(x-{\\color{blue}2})$ is a *factor* of the polynomial. Indeed, it can be shown that this polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll learn more about factoring polynomials using the factor theorem in future lessons. But for now, we can check that this is correct by multiplying the parentheses." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ffefc48-3af9-5767-83c4-bf215db91563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3719 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "848515fd-f525-5e59-ac3a-e5f8bb64943f" :step/math-academy-id 20941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087571 :question/id #uuid "01993d51-8a9f-5156-b5cc-2784e93da5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522746}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "cd1ba65e-8d34-5dd7-89fc-a3588ee3811c" :step/math-academy-id 20942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088934 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the factor theorem to factors of the form $(ax-b).$\n\nFor example, suppose that $(2x-3)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x).$ Then, we can immediately obtain a root of $p(x)$ by setting the factor equal to $0$ and solving for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, it follows that \n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this more general form of the factor theorem as follows:\n\n- If $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x),$ then $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\n- Conversely, if $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x),$ then $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of $p(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd34f044-e27c-5559-bb43-7d385325b69e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3720 :tutorial/title "The Factor Theorem for General Linear Factors"} :step/id #uuid "4e0fd1fd-b17d-5ee1-99f9-799218de5423" :step/math-academy-id 20943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522791}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b6addca5-a5ce-5e5a-ba37-b179ad6bd1f6" :step/math-academy-id 20944}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087917}]}
